    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Known mostly from Catholic opponents, the Cathars are described as holding that a good God of the spiritual realm stood against an evil god who made the material world, in which souls were trapped until released by the consolamentum.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>, begun in 1209, and then the Inquisition, set up in 1233, ended the movement; thousands were killed, among them the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a> and more than 200 Perfects burned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montsegur">Montségur</a> in 1244.</li><li>Since the 1990s some historians, notably R. I. Moore, have questioned whether Catharism was an organised religion at all, while others hold that the evidence shows a real heresy with a theology of its own.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>On 13 May 1239 in Champagne, 183 men and women convicted of Catharism were burned at the stake on the orders of the Dominican inquisitor Robert le Bougre, himself a former Cathar Perfect.</p></aside>
    </section>

