    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The origins of Cathar beliefs are unclear. Most 20th-century historians agreed that they came from the East along trade routes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, and that the movement was greatly influenced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomilism">Bogomils</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, perhaps through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulicians">Paulicians</a> resettled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. Mark Pegg and others warn against inferring direct historical links from similarities that modern scholars perceive. Dimitri Obolensky and Steven Runciman proposed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> and early Gnostic movements; this view has been widely rejected since primary Manichaean texts were found, though traditional Catholic scholarship still associates the Cathars with Manichaeism.</p><p>Most scholars agree that identifiable Catharism did not emerge before 1143, when the cleric Eberwin of Steinfeld reported a group with similar beliefs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. A council held in 1167 at Saint-Félix-Lauragais was attended by local figures, the Bogomil papa Nicetas, the Cathar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and a leader of the Cathars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>.</p><p>The present debate has two frameworks: transmission through the Byzantine Empire, argued by Malcolm Barber and Janet and Bernard Hamilton, or an independent local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Christian development unrelated to any dualist heresy, argued by Robert Ian Moore and Mark Gregory Pegg.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Few Cathar texts survive, so most of what is known comes from Catholic denunciations. According to them, Cathars believed in two Gods: the good God of Heaven, creator of the spiritual realm, and an evil god, often called Rex Mundi, who made the visible world. Some communities held a mitigated dualism in which Satan was once the true God’s servant; others, probably a majority over time, held an absolute dualism of two equal powers. Human souls were seen as angels seduced by Satan and trapped in matter, reincarnated until they renounced the material self.</p><p>Cathars venerated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and called themselves Good Christians, but denied his physical incarnation and resurrection; authors compare their view with Docetism. They rejected the cross as an instrument of torture, saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>’s resurrection as a symbol of reincarnation, and denied transubstantiation, purgatory and prayers to saints. Some communities expected a Day of Judgment.</p><p>Their one central rite was the consolamentum, which removed all sin and made the believer a Perfect. They ate no meat, cheese, eggs or milk, as products of sexual intercourse, but ate fish, believed to arise by spontaneous generation. War and capital punishment were condemned, and reproduction was seen as a moral evil.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Few writings of the Cathars survive, because most were destroyed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a> judged their doctrine a threat, and so the record rests mainly on their opponents. Beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, their alleged sacred texts included the Bogomil Gospel of the Secret Supper, also called John’s Interrogation, and a modified version of the Ascension of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a>. They regarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> as written by Satan, apart from a few books they accepted, and read the Book of Revelation not as prophecy but as an allegorical chronicle of Satan’s rebellion.</p><p>One large Cathar work survives, The Book of Two Principles (Liber de duobus principiis), possibly written by the Italian Cathar John Lugio of Bergamo. It sets out dualist theology from the viewpoint of some Albanenses Cathars. A few other texts survive in their opponents’ hands, such as the Rituel Cathare de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>. The Cathars held that scripture should be read in the vernacular.</p><p>The Fournier Register, made when Jacques Fournier, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Pamiers, had suspected heretics interrogated around Montaillou, records their words through scribes. It was edited by Jean Duvernoy and is the basis of Emmanuel Le Roy Ladurie’s Montaillou.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/montsegur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montsegur.jpg" alt="Montségur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/montsegur">Montségur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montsegur.p%C3%B2g1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Obra pròpia, cedeixo tots els drets (domini públic), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cathars sprang up in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> cities, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, and in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the mid-12th century, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> and northern Italian cities in the mid to late 12th century. In the Languedoc and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> they attained their greatest popularity. By 1207 many towns of Provence and its vicinity were almost wholly Cathar. Missions sent from 1147, among them those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a> and of Henry of Marcy, won only momentary successes.</p><p>In January 1208 the legate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-de-castelnau">Pierre de Castelnau</a> was murdered, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> ordered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> preached. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> lasted twenty years. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a>, besieged on 22 July 1209, crusaders killed at least 7,000 people according to reports, though some scholars dispute the number. Arnaud Amalric wrote that twenty thousand were put to the sword, a figure scholars dispute as figurative. At Muret in 1213 Simon de Montfort defeated the allied army of Raymond of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-aragon">Peter II of Aragon</a>, who died there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1229">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1229 ended the war.</p><p>The Inquisition, established in 1233, crushed Catharism as a popular movement. Over 200 Perfects were burned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montsegur">Montségur</a> on 16 March 1244. Peire Autier, leader of a revival in the Pyrenean foothills, was executed in 1310, and the last known Perfect of the Languedoc, Guillaume Bélibaste, in 1321.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cathars called themselves Good Men, Good Women or Good Christians; the name Cathar was coined by Catholic theologians, and whether the movement used it is debated. It is alleged that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> church had a flat structure, with the Perfecti, whom they called bonhommes, and unbaptised believers, the credentes. Bishoprics were created at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albi">Albi</a> around 1165, then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agen">Agen</a>, four by 1200, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/razes">Razès</a> about 1225. Perfecti lived austerely and travelled in pairs.</p><p>Women could be Perfects and administer the consolamentum, so Catharism has been seen as giving women the greatest opportunities for independent action. Women Perfects had their own houses, and communal homes taught women the faith. Yet some Cathars held that salvation required a last incarnation as a man, and the late Cathars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> began to exclude women Perfects, though Italian Perfects still included them.</p><p>The Cathars had little hostility to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and appointed some as bailiffs and public officials. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Fulk of Toulouse rebuked the Languedoc knights for not pursuing heretics, they replied that they had been reared among them and had relatives among them. Repentant lay believers were spared but had to sew yellow crosses onto their clothing; those who refused to recant or relapsed were hanged or burned.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Since the 1990s the existence of Catharism as an institutional religion has been contested. R. I. Moore argues in The War on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heresy">Heresy</a> that it was largely contrived in the imaginations of churchmen from scattered local anticlericalism and a broader spiritual revival, and some scholars have called the fear of Cathars a moral panic comparable with the witch-hunts. Claire Taylor and Rebecca Rist reply that evidence shows a real heresy, and Lucy Sackville argues that it had an organised theology. Cathars in Question (2016), edited by Antonio Sennis, gathered the conflicting views.</p><p>Later readers reinterpreted the Cathars. John Foxe and Jean Duvernoy saw proto-Protestant theology in them, a reading many historians criticised, and some 20th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptists">Baptists</a> claimed them as forerunners, which Dwight Longenecker rejects and Baptist historiography has largely left. In the 1930s Otto Rahn linked them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-grail">Holy Grail</a>, and popular culture has linked them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>. The Waldensians, who suffered persecution in the same area, survive today.</p><p>Today the Cathar castles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montsegur">Montségur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a> stand in what the Aude calls Pays cathare. The Occitan song Lo Boièr is particularly associated with Catharism, and its legacy is also seen in the troubadours’ poems and songs.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Catharism was a Christian dualist movement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from the 12th to the 14th century, known mostly through Catholic accounts. It was attacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> from 1209 and by the Inquisition, which claimed to have eradicated it by 1350. Historians still debate how organised it was.</em></p>

